## THE DECISIONS INSIDE THIS KIT (each Tuesday's as coordinator; each a Question)
- **D1 TWO PROJECTS, NO THIRD:** `src/Datasec.Suite.Logging/Datasec.Suite.Logging.csproj` (class library; namespace root `Datasec.Suite.Logging`; the scanner lives in it under `Datasec.Suite.Logging.Scanning`) and `tests/Datasec.Suite.Logging.Tests/Datasec.Suite.Logging.Tests.csproj`. The name is the one the repository's README reserves (MEASURED [F3]: `README.md:53`). No console project, no tool, no script under `build/`.
- **D2 TWO PACKAGES, PINNED EXACT:** `Serilog` `[4.4.0]` and `Serilog.Sinks.File` `[7.0.0]`. Where the versions come from: the newest non-prerelease release tags of the two public source repositories, read by `gh api -X GET` at 17:51:34 (MEASURED [F5]: `serilog/serilog` tag `v4.4.0` published 2026-07-10; `serilog/serilog-sinks-file` tag `v7.0.0` published 2025-04-28). **That the package feed holds a package of exactly that version under exactly that id is DERIVED from the tag and UNMEASURED (the drafter may call no host but GitHub): your restore is the proof, and a restore that cannot find one is a STOP with the tool's line, never a different version.** The JSON form of an event comes from the formatter inside the core package (UNMEASURED that 4.4.0 holds one: if it does not, STOP; a third package is Q4's alternative, not your choice).
- **D3 THE SIX CLASSES OF `02:567`, AS DATA IN ONE FILE:** token, session handle, card string, mail content, file name, password. Each class holds name stems. **The stems are this kit's, not the design's (the design names classes, no field name; no other lane's code exists yet to take names from):** token: `token`, `authorization`, `secret`; session handle: `sessionhandle`, `handle`; card string: `cardstring`, `cardnumber`, `carddata`, `cardid`, `rawcard`; mail content: `subject`, `body`, `mailcontent`, `messagecontent`; file name: `filename`, `filepath`, `documentname`; password: `password`, `passwd`, `pwd`, `passphrase`. You may ADD a stem you can argue from a line of the design (name the line in STATUS); you remove none.
- **D4 THE MATCHING RULE ERRS TOWARD REMOVING:** a property name is normalised (lower case; every character that is not a letter or a digit dropped) and is on the list when the normalised name CONTAINS a stem. So `AccessToken`, `access_token`, `X-Refresh-Token` and `userAccessTokenValue` are all on it, and so is `TokenCount` (a harmless number is lost: the accepted cost). The quiet control is a name that holds no stem (`Outcome`).
- **D5 THE VALUE IS REPLACED, THE NAME STAYS:** a listed property keeps its name and carries the fixed text `[removed:<class>]` (for example `[removed:token]`) in place of its value, whatever the value's type. **This text will be read by whoever reads a log or a support bundle: it is listed for Tuesday as a possibly customer-facing word (Q5); you invent no other wording.**
- **D6 THE LIST CANNOT BE BYPASSED BY CONFIGURATION:** the library offers no way to build a logger without the list, no setting, environment variable or level that switches it off, and no public way to add a sink in front of it. (The tests reach a logger WITHOUT the list through an `internal` seam, visible to the test assembly only, as the Core project does for its tests: MEASURED [F3], `InternalsVisibleTo` at `src/Datasec.Suite.Core/Datasec.Suite.Core.csproj:3`.)
- **D7 THE SCANNER IS VALUE-FIRST:** it is given the folder and the list of values that must not appear (the test knows what it used); it searches each value as is, JSON-string-escaped, percent-encoded, and as standard and URL-safe base64 of its UTF-8 bytes. ONE shape rule needs no value: the compact form of a JSON Web Token (three base64url parts joined by two dots, the first of which decodes to a JSON object), which is RFC 7519's form and says nothing about any vendor's tokens. **No rule of yours states what a token of a Microsoft service, a card string of any reader or a handle of any printer looks like: that would be an invented fact (`<project>/CLAUDE.md:61-62`).**
- **D8 A SCAN THAT READ NOTHING IS NOT CLEAN:** 0 files read, or a file that is not valid UTF-8, or a file it cannot open, is a finding, never a skip (the rule G3 of the release guard, MEASURED [F3] `README.md:66`, applied here).
- **D9 THE SCANNER NEVER REPEATS WHAT IT FOUND:** a finding names the file, the line number, the rule or the INDEX of the value, and the form; never the value, never the line's text.
- **D10 NO JOB STEP, NO FLOOR CHANGE, NO FINGERPRINT CHANGE** (section SHARED FILES).
- **D11 ONE COMMIT** on the branch, at most ONE more if the hosted run is red at a cause you can fix; then STOP.
- **D12 YOU WRITE ONE SHARED RECORDS FILE, `5_Project_History/history.md`** (the project's rule, `<project>/CLAUDE.md:4-6`), by one read-prepend-replace in one process; `BACKLOG.md` and `SETUP_RUNBOOK.md` are NOT yours in this wave: what they should gain goes into STATUS, section FOR RECORDS.

