## THE TWO PROPERTIES AND THEIR CASES (closed lists, by PROPERTY first)
**Property P1, the deny-list.** For every logger the library builds, at every level, no sink receives the value of a property whose name is on the list, wherever a structured event holds a value: as the property itself, inside an object or a collection the event carries at any depth, in the rendered message, in the JSON line and in a text line. A property not on the list arrives unchanged. **At least these cases (14 rows; any other place a value can ride: prove it is covered or add a row and say so):**
| Case | What the test logs (every value SYNTHETIC, section below) | Required |
|---|---|---|
| L01 | a scalar under a listed name, one per class (six sub-cases) | the value is in NO sink's output; the fixed text of D5 with the right class is there instead |
| L02 | the same event rendered as text (the message with its properties filled in) | the value is not in the rendered message |
| L03 | an object logged with its structure kept, holding a listed member at depth 1 | the member's value is gone; its sibling members arrive unchanged |
| L04 | the same at depth 3 | as L03 |
| L05 | a dictionary with a listed KEY | that entry's value is gone; the other entries arrive |
| L06 | a sequence of objects, the second of which holds a listed member | as L03, for that element |
| L07 | a listed property attached to the logger's context (not passed in the message) | gone in every event that carries it |
| L08 | the names of D4: mixed case, underscores, a hyphen, a prefix and a suffix around a stem | each is treated as listed |
| L09 | every stem of D3, read from the list file itself by the test (the test does not carry its own copy of the stems) | each stem, used alone as a name, is treated as listed; the test prints how many stems it read and fails on 0 |
| L10 | the JSON line and the text line of one event, both | the value is in neither |
| L11 | QUIET: a property named `Outcome` and a property named `DurationMs` | both arrive unchanged in every sink (the list does not simply drop everything) |
| L12 | a `null` value, an empty string, a number and a byte array under a listed name | no exception reaches the caller; no part of the value arrives |
| L13 | the events of L01 at the most verbose level, with the minimum level set to the most verbose | as L01 (`02:165`: the debug switch's output is under the list) |
| L14 | a logger with TWO sinks (the file and an in-memory collector added through the library) | both receive the same cleaned event: nothing sits in front of the list |
**Property P2, the scanner.** Given a folder of log files and the values that must not appear, the scan reports a finding for every occurrence of a value in any form of D7 and for every token-shaped string of D7, reports how many files and lines it read, treats what it could not read as a finding, and never repeats a value. **At least these cases (10 rows):**
| Case | The input | Required |
|---|---|---|
| S01 | **THE PLANTED MARKER (`03:88`):** a log folder written by the library, into ONE file of which the test plants a marker value on a line of its own | exactly 1 finding, at that file and line; this case runs in the SAME test run as S02, so a green S02 never stands without it |
| S02 | the same folder without the plant | 0 findings, files read at least 1, lines read at least 1 |
| S03 | a known value in each of the five forms of D7, one file per form | 1 finding per file, each naming its form |
| S04 | a token-shaped string that is NOT among the known values | 1 finding by the shape rule |
| S05 | QUIET for the shape rule: a dotted version `1.2.3`, a host name of three labels, a file name `a.b.c` | 0 findings |
| S06 | an EMPTY folder; a folder that does not exist | a finding or an error, never a clean result |
| S07 | a file that is not valid UTF-8; a file saved as UTF-16 | a finding per file (unreadable), never a skip |
| S08 | **THE LEAK NO FIELD NAME STOPS:** a value written through the library under a name that is NOT on the list (`Detail`), and the same value inside the message of a logged exception | the deny-list lets both through (assert that: it is the limit of P1, said plainly) and the scan of the folder FINDS both |
| S09 | **E1.8 IN UNIT FORM:** a token, a card string and a file name (and one value of each other class) logged under their listed names through the library to a real file; the logger disposed; the folder scanned with those values as the known values | 0 findings, with the files-read and lines-read counts printed; and the SAME events through the `internal` seam WITHOUT the list: one finding or more per value (this is S09's own red) |
| S10 | the output of every scan above | holds none of the values and none of the lines' text |
**Known-value hygiene:** the scanner refuses a known value shorter than 8 characters (it would match everywhere) with an error, never silently (one test).
**The limits you NAME and do not close (in the README-free place you own: an XML doc comment on the scanner's entry type, and STATUS):** a value split over two lines; a value in a compressed or rotated-and-compressed file (it is reported unreadable, not read); base64 of a value at another byte offset inside a longer encoded text; hexadecimal and any other encoding; a value shorter than 8 characters; a value inside the message of an exception or under an unlisted name is stopped by NOTHING before the sink, only seen by the scan afterwards.
**Mutants (the builder's own run, not independent; each made with the Edit tool in `<clone>`, the tests run under the lock, then undone with the Edit tool and proved by `git -C '<clone>' diff --quiet` against the staged tree):** M1 the list emptied (every class without stems): L01, L08, L09 and S09 go RED. M2 the walk into objects and collections removed: L03 to L06 go RED and L01 stays green. M3 the scanner's value search returns nothing: S01, S03, S08 go RED. M4 the scanner accepts 0 files as clean: S06 goes RED. M5 the plant of S01 removed from the test: S01 goes RED (the positive control proves itself). Print per mutant the RED test names and the summary line; a mutant that leaves every test green is a hole: close it with a test and say so.

