7. **THE RECORDS.** `<project>/5_Project_History/history.md`: ONE new entry directly under the title line, above the existing entries, dated by `date`, saying what the commit holds and that the host is not wired; it names no person and no decision. **Other seats write this file in the same hours:** ONE program reads the file, builds the whole new content, writes a temp file beside it and `os.replace`s it, then re-reads the file and proves (i) your entry is there once and (ii) everything that was below the title line when it read is still there byte for byte, in order; if the file's bytes changed between its own read and its replace (compare a hash taken at each end), it starts again from the read. Encode the ADDED bytes as ASCII and check them; never re-encode the whole file. Then STATUS, section FOR RECORDS: the sentences `SETUP_RUNBOOK.md`, `BACKLOG.md` and the repository's `README.md` should gain (the README's two lists; the lock; the clone; the limits you named), for a records pass to file.
8. **CONTEXT BAND AND HANDOVER.** Long files are read with `offset` and `limit` in pages of at most 100 lines; a Read refusal is a page-size message, not the context band. About 70% context (the statusline's figure, quoted): start no new part; about 75%: stop at a safe boundary (files whole, no process of yours running, the lock released, any mutant undone and proved, nothing half-staged) and write `<project>/1_Project_Definition/Briefs/2026-11-20_B1787_HANDOVER.md` (done / in hand / next / processes left / decisions a successor keeps / your base and head), last line `HANDOVER B1787: next <step> | done <steps>`, and end STATUS with the same line.
